---
import '../styles/style.css';
import '../styles/themes.css';
import '../styles/themes-generated.css';
const base = (import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '')) + '/';
---
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
  <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=IBM+Plex+Serif:wght@300;400&family=IBM+Plex+Sans:wght@300;400;500&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500&family=Space+Grotesk:wght@300;400;500;700&family=Inter:wght@300;400;700;900&family=VT323&family=Playfair+Display:wght@400;700;900&family=Orbitron:wght@400;700;900&family=Cormorant+Garamond:wght@300;400;600&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;500&family=Source+Serif+4:wght@300;400&family=Archivo+Black&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
  <title>Side Channel Cloud</title>
  <script is:inline>
    document.documentElement.dataset.theme = localStorage.getItem('scc-theme') || 'amodo-dark';
  </script>
</head>
<body data-base={base}>
  <div class="page-frame"></div>
  <div class="progress" id="progress"></div>

  <header class="topbar">
    <div class="brand"><span class="glyph"></span> <b>SIDE&nbsp;CHANNEL</b> <span>// CLOUD</span></div>
    <nav class="navlinks">
      <a href={base} class="active" data-n="01">Landing</a>
      <a href={base + 'gallery/'} data-n="02">Dev Model Picker</a>
      <a href={base + 'explorer/'} data-n="03">Explorer</a>
      <a href="https://amododesign.com/" class="ext" data-n="//">Amodo Design</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <!-- pinned right column: wireframe + (during the hero) the access card -->
  <div class="scroll-stage stage-dim" id="stage">
    <canvas id="stage-canvas"></canvas>

    <aside class="access-card show" id="access-card">
      <span class="tag">Early access</span>
      <h3>Get access to the side-channel cloud</h3>
      <form id="access-form">
        <label>Name
          <input type="text" name="name" autocomplete="name" required />
        </label>
        <label>Email
          <input type="email" name="email" autocomplete="email" required />
        </label>
        <label>Company <span class="opt">(optional)</span>
          <input type="text" name="company" autocomplete="organization" />
        </label>
        <div class="check-row">
          <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="access" value="free" /> Free access</label>
          <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="access" value="paid" /> Paid access</label>
        </div>
        <button class="cta-btn" type="submit">Get Access Today →</button>
      </form>
      <div class="access-success">
        <h3>You're on the list.</h3>
        <p>We'll be in touch when the next hardware wave opens. Meanwhile,
           pick the dev model you want to work against.</p>
        <a class="btn" href={base + 'gallery/'}>Open the Dev Model Picker →</a>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <!-- scrolling copy on the left -->
  <main class="scroll-content">

    <section class="section hero" data-stage="form" data-tags="" data-xray="false">
      <span class="tag">Bare-metal GPU · scopes · SDRs · TAPs</span>
      <h1>Side Channel <em>Cloud</em></h1>
      <p>AI infrastructure for your AI security and verification work. If bare
         metal isn't enough access for your research, then the side channel
         cloud is for you. Our AI cluster is instrumented with scopes, SDRs, TAPs,
         and more to enable your most ambitious hardware R&amp;D.</p>
      <p>And if you're working on open-source or non-profit work, you can get
         completely free access — already paid up for by research funders.</p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn" href={base + 'gallery/'}>Dev Model Picker →</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section explainer" data-stage="hidden" data-tags="" data-xray="false">
      <div class="explainer-card">
        <span class="tag">01 — Mission</span>
        <h2>Why we exist</h2>
        <p class="lead">AI security and verification need <b>100×</b> more technical
           R&amp;D — but hardware access makes this infeasible for most academic,
           non-profit, or independent research groups.</p>
        <p>We're a hardware R&amp;D lab working on the 21st century's most important
           technologies. Whilst running our AI security and verification programmes,
           we were repeatedly bottlenecked by a lack of hands-on access to frontier
           AI accelerators (H100s, H200s, +). We've partnered with research funders
           to end this bottleneck. The side channel cloud is a research cluster
           instrumented to the gills with side-channel monitoring instruments —
           the perfect setup for researchers who need in-depth hardware access but
           can't host millions of dollars of compute with building-scale power
           requirements.</p>
        <p>We have standard tray and rack setups, plus we can adapt setups to suit
           you. Need an FPGA added? Want an SDR in a different place? We can
           reconfigure the setup to meet your needs.</p>
        <div class="refs">
          <span class="refs-label">The reading that motivates this — RAND papers &amp; substacks</span>
          <a class="ref rand" href="https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_reports/RRA2849-1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>RAND</b><span>Securing AI Model Weights · RR-A2849</span></a>
          <a class="ref rand" href="https://www.rand.org/pubs/working_papers/WRA3056-1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>RAND</b><span>Hardware-Enabled Governance Mechanisms · WR-A3056</span></a>
          <a class="ref cnas" href="https://www.cnas.org/publications/reports/secure-governable-chips" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>CNAS</b><span>Secure, Governable Chips</span></a>
          <a class="ref arxiv" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.16074" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>arXiv</b><span>2408.16074 · Verification for international AI agreements</span></a>
          <a class="ref substack" href="https://importai.substack.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>Substack</b><span>Import AI — Jack Clark</span></a>
          <a class="ref substack" href="https://www.hyperdimensional.co/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>Substack</b><span>Hyperdimensional — Dean Ball</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" data-tags="power,busbar" data-xray="true">
      <span class="tag">02 — Power side channels</span>
      <h2>Power <em>side channels</em>.</h2>
      <p>We've instrumented the DC bus to our H200 GPUs. We've already used it to
         tell whether a GPU is running training or inference, and our collaborators
         have used it to identify which model is running on a GPU.</p>
      <div class="refs">
        <span class="refs-label">Similar research</span>
        <a class="ref arxiv" href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.11894" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>arXiv</b><span>DeepTheft — stealing DNN architectures via power (S&amp;P '24)</span></a>
        <a class="ref rg" href="https://www.researchgate.net" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>RG</b><span>A New Foe in GPUs — power side-channel attacks on NNs</span></a>
        <a class="ref arxiv" href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.14551" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>arXiv</b><span>Empirically-calibrated H100 node power models (2025)</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" data-tags="nic,interconnect" data-xray="true">
      <span class="tag">03 — Network TAPs</span>
      <h2>Network <em>TAPs</em>.</h2>
      <p>Many AI verification regimes rely on network TAPs — devices that let a
         verifier observe a prover's network packets. We can place TAPs anywhere
         on the network (speed permitting) to enable your work.</p>
      <div class="refs">
        <span class="refs-label">Further reading</span>
        <a class="ref amodo" href="https://amododesign.com/notes/2026-05-03-network-tapping/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>Amodo</b><span>Note · Network tapping</span></a>
        <a class="ref substack" href="https://nacicankaya.substack.com/p/research-note-the-fundamentals-and" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>Substack</b><span>Naci Cankaya · Fundamentals of network TAPs</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" data-tags="memory,gpu" data-xray="true">
      <span class="tag">04 — Software-defined radios</span>
      <h2>Software-defined <em>radios</em>.</h2>
      <p>Every accelerator leaks. Clocks, DRAM, voltage regulators and data buses
         all emit electromagnetic energy that correlates with the computation
         underneath — and unlike a power tap, EM emanations can often be captured
         without physical contact.</p>
      <p>We position SDRs around the cluster, and we can move probes and antennas
         to wherever your experiment needs them.</p>
      <div class="refs">
        <span class="refs-label">Similar research</span>
        <a class="ref arxiv" href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.02891" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>arXiv</b><span>Kraken — higher-order EM attacks on DNNs, near &amp; far field (2026)</span></a>
        <a class="ref arxiv" href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.00579" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>arXiv</b><span>Revealing CNN architectures via side-channel analysis (2023)</span></a>
        <a class="ref arxiv" href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.09717" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>arXiv</b><span>Deep-TEMPEST — EM HDMI eavesdropping with deep learning (2024)</span></a>
        <a class="ref conf" href="https://www.usenix.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>USENIX</b><span>Snooping the GPU via magnetic side channel (Sec '22)</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" data-tags="cpu,pcb,drive" data-xray="true">
      <span class="tag">05 — Bare-metal access</span>
      <h2>Bare-metal <em>access</em>.</h2>
      <p>With deep system access you can run detailed system profiling. Want to
         research GPU hardware counters? We'll set up a machine with the access
         to enable it.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" data-tags="connector,cable" data-xray="true">
      <span class="tag">06 — Reconfigurable hardware</span>
      <h2>Other reconfigurable <em>hardware</em>.</h2>
      <p>We can drop programmable hardware into the data path wherever you need it —
         BlueField DPUs, FPGAs, SmartNICs, custom switches. Whatever the setup, we
         can accommodate it.</p>
      <div class="refs">
        <span class="refs-label">From our notebook</span>
        <a class="ref amodo" href="https://amododesign.com/notes/2026-03-16-dpu-bandwidth-limiter/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><b>Amodo</b><span>Note · Tray-level bandwidth limits with BlueField DPUs</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="provision" data-tags="" data-xray="false">
      <span class="tag">07 — Get started</span>
      <h2>Ready when <em>you are</em>.</h2>
      <p>Register your interest at the top of the page — free for open-source and
         non-profit research — or browse the standard tray and rack setups first.</p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn" href="#top" onclick="window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'});return false;">Get access ↑</a>
        <a class="btn" href={base + 'gallery/'}>Dev Model Picker →</a>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>

  <script>
    import '../scripts/scroll-site.js';
  </script>
</body>
</html>
